Milestone One
Purpose	
IVAs or Intelligent Virtual Assistants will be used a lot in the future. It is important that we understand both the risks and benefits that will come with using them. 
Summary of IVA features
	Intelligent Virtual Assistant (IVA) is an advanced AI-enabled chat assistant that can engage in personalized conversations using analytics and cognitive computing (Uniphore, 2024). IVAs use natural language processing and understanding to interpret how humans talk including things such as slang, context, and misspellings. They also use machine learning to respond more accurately over time by analyzing past interactions. Another cool thing that IVAs do is personalize answers to individuals. 
Underlying technology with security implications
	IVAs understand text and voice commands, perform automated tasks like scheduling meetings, and can answer questions. This can make them excellent for customer service reasons. They can also be used to control a house remotely. They can adjust thermostats, play music, or even turn on or off lights. This can be very helpful if you are on vacation and want to make it appear like you are home.
	These are great benefits but also pose major security risks. Sometimes devices can think you are talking to them when you don’t mean to and record private details about your life. These files are saved on the cloud and are targets for data breaches. There are also often built in app trackers that can gather data to be sold without you knowing. These virtual assistants might have access to a lot of your personal things such as lights and house locks. Having so much being taken care of by one assistant can mean that if it gets hacked the hacker can control a lot of things. 
Security risks of natural language processing
	Security risks will increase as conversations between people and their virtual assistants as the assistants become better at interacting with humans in a way that seems human. These IVAs will start to sound real and friendly. This makes people willing to trust them and share secrets that they shouldn’t. Machines can also be biased because of their training. They can share this bias without it being obvious that they are doing so. They are also not human and may offer bad advice without knowing that it is bad. 
	When helping customers Sonya will ask certain questions and store the answers. However, customers may offer information that Sonya doesn’t require. This information would probably also be stored in the cloud, meaning that it could be hacked. It would be very difficult to try and get Sonya to separate information that is needed from information that is and to get rid of anything that it doesn’t require to help the customer.
Two Adversarial attack examples
	Intelligent Virtual Machines can be the target of several types of adversarial attacks. A lot of classic cybersecurity threats have been adapted to work against IVAs. These include things such as Cross-site scripting and social engineering. A hacker could hide instructions into pdfs or emails that the intelligent virtual machine processes. This tricks the IVA into doing things it shouldn’t like having people it works with download viruses or go to fake websites. The IVA doesn’t even know that it has done something it shouldn’t, which makes it difficult to notice for whoever is meant to monitor the IVA. This type of attack is similar to phishing but instead of tricking humans to click malicious links in tricks the IVAs into doing it.
	Cross-Site scripting is another type of adversarial attack to watch out for. The old method of doing this was to inject malicious scripts into trusted websites. Then a browser would execute them using a different user’s session. The IVA adaptation of this is to embed trigger phrases or malicious instructions into external data sources. When the IVA looks at these files the injected information runs and forces the assistant to do things it shouldn’t such as sending unauthorized emails or changing system settings.
	Another adversarial attack that is common is hiding macro viruses. The old method would hide these tiny viruses in things such as documents, and they would run themselves when the file was open. Then they would infect any adjacent files. The new method is where AI agents have a command injected into them and then when they respond to messages or create new content it injects the same malicious prompt that it was injected with. In this way it spreads without anyone having to do anything to spread it.
Control Measures Explanation
	There are several different control measures that can be taken to try and protect against these attacks. For cross-site scripting when it comes to IVAs use Data/instruction isolation to fix the issue. This control forces the AI to treat prompts from user as passive data and not as executable commands. Output filtering will also help to keep Sonya safe from cross-site scripting by scanning any text that is generated for any script tags and getting rid of them before executing anything on the browser.
	Social engineering is a huge issue to watch for. This is when hackers trick humans into trusting them and executing unauthorized actions such as transferring funds. There are a few ways we can solve this problem. The first is we could set it up so that if Sonya gets tricked into performing an unauthorized action the system will halt until a human can manually approve the action. Another way we could stop this is by using Dual-LLM architecture. This non-human gatekeeper that is trained specifically to spot social engineering things such as urgency cues and deceptive language and stopping it before a human ever interacts with it.
	Macro viruses hide executable code by embedding it into files that should seem safe such as word or excel files. Once these files are opened the code is executed and copied into adjacent files. A way to control this is by using network micro segmentation. This segments files from each other so that if a virus is executed on a device it blocks any communication to other files.
Control measure evaluation
	Certain control measures are much easier to deploy such as User confirmation interceptors and Data/Instruction isolation via prompt engineering. The reason we should start with these is that they are low cost and highly available. They also stop high-risk autonomous threats such as email exfiltration and unauthorized data deletion. 
	 Once we have the above control measures implemented, we can move on to ones that cost more but have a good overall return on investment. We should start with Behavioral EDR/XDR next. This would be helpful because it immediately kills processes that it considers to be illegal system behavior such as an office application trying to open a cmd.exe file. Behavioral EDR does require the software to be purchased up front, but it can protect against both traditional malware and modern AI-mutated code. 
	After that we should get a Dual-LLM gatekeeper. This also is supposed to have a really good return on investment. Dual-LLM gatekeepers don’t use a lot of bandwidth and protect against injection attacks. With the advancement of AI more cyber threats appear but the controls I have talked about should be a great starting point to keep Sonya Safe.
	The ideal state that I think we should work towards is the Zero-Trust Semantic Architecture State. In this state virtual assistants operate under continuous verification. This means that everything the virtual assistant deals with such as inputs and outputs is scrutinized by automatic security layers that make sure it is safe before executing anything.
