	I added a section about sharing data with third parties. Sometimes hackers have gained access to businesses through third parties. I wanted to assure our customers that the third parties we work with are subject to the same requirements that we are to keep data safe. I also changed the marketing section a little bit by explaining a bit about what kind of marketing we may do. I also wanted them to know that they could opt out of marketing communications. The individuals having the knowledge that they can choose to stop getting marketing communications makes me feel like they would be more willing to give it a try.
Justification Under GDPR
	The revised privacy statement goes into a lot more detail that shows that it follows the principles of the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR). These principles include how data is stored, processed, collected, and shared. The original privacy policy only talked about very general information that doesn’t prove that it follows the GDPR at all. With the updates I made to the policy it is easy to see that it follows the GDPR principles of lawfulness, fairness and transparency. This does so by explaining why we collect and process identifying personal data. This updated version also explains how individuals can exercise their privacy rights. By providing this information, it shows customers that Callego is using the data in a transparent manner. I also address purpose limitations. This states that Callego will only use personal data for legitimate business purposes. The revised statement also shows how we are using data minimization by talking about only collecting the data that is adequate, relevant and necessary for its stated purposes.
Description of impacts on Organization
	The revised privacy policy should have a positive impact on Callegos’ business. Having statements that clearly describe what we do with their personal information is a good way to gain trust from the customers. Explaining some of the ways that we protect the information should help them believe that we have their best interests at heart. The better explanation of marketing should also get more people to sign up for newletters and other things we offer. They know that they can always unsubscribe if they don’t like the information they are receiving. Offering customers choices is another good way to help build Callegos reputation. 
	Callego would need to implement specific administrative, and technical controls to help with GDPR compliance. They need to do this to ensure that they can keep all promises they made in the privacy statement. One of these controls should be an identity and access management control based on the principles of least privilege. Callego also needs to make sure they have the ability to encrypt data both when it is in rest and in transit. We should also implement a security information and event management system to help keep everything safe. We also need to make sure that we have written policies to make sure that all the employees are following the GDPR principles. By doing all of these things I believe Callego will have a great reputation and continue to grow as a company.
Milestone Two part B
Identification and Description of Recommended Control Measures
	Since we now have an accelerated time schedule, we need to add control measures that are both meaningful and relatively quick to implement. These controls also need to be good for long term GDPR compliance. The GDPR principles focus on fairness, lawfulness, and transparency.
	The first control measure I recommend adding is a security monitoring and endpoint detection and response system (SIEM/EDR). The next control I would recommend is to have Role-based access control along with multifactor authentication. This sounds like two controls, but I think rolling it into one makes sense in most cases. The last thing we should do right now is to make sure we have good encryption and data loss prevention controls in place.
	A SIEM/EDR monitors all the systems on a network. It does so by collecting security logs from important systems such as firewalls, applications, and authentication servers. It looks for suspicious activity and then will alert the cybersecurity team if it finds anything suspicious. The EDR looks at individual workstations and servers and can identify malware, unauthorized access, unusual file activity, and other indicators of compromise. Both of these together allow Callego to see both the broader issues on the network and the issues with individual workstations as well.
	Role-based access control is something I would recommend for all companies. This means that each employee at the business only has access to what they need to do their jobs. This means that if a hacker got control of that person’s account, they could only access a very limited number of things on the network. Multi-factor authentication also works well with role-based access. Multi-factor authentication means that it requires at least two things before accessing an account such as a password and a code sent in a text. Together these things make it harder to get into an account and limit what can be done if a hacker does get in.
	The last control is encryption and data-loss prevention. Encryption needs to be set up so that it protects the data whether it is at rest or in transmit. This means that databases, laptops, and any other system that contains private data is encrypted. Any communications that take place should also be encrypted. Data loss prevention goes right along with this. This control identifies and tries to control when data is being improperly moved.
Present and Future State Description
	Callego already generates some system and application logs but doesn’t have a central way to review them. We have so far been reviewing these logs manually. This is not as effective as having a SIEM that can see them all in one place and lock through the logs for us. In the future state Callego has SIEM working so we don’t have to waste time manually pulling up logs. They are all in one place and we are informed about things such as multiple failed login attempts and logins from unusual locations. We should continue to move forward by continuing to update what we want our SIEM to look for based on historical data.
	Callego currently has a password policy that requires passwords to be at least ten characters, have a capital and lowercase letter, and at least one number or symbol. This is a great starting point as it makes sure that passwords are relatively strong. We also have basic access controls in place. When someone takes a job the IT department gives them access to certain things. However, people often need to work with others and end up with access to other things to help them with their work. This extra access is often left on the accounts. This causes people with the same role to have access to different things, and it isn’t monitored very well.
	In the future state we need to make sure that each role has the same access and that if extra access is needed that it is only granted temporarily. Having these defined access roles will help to keep data safe. We also need to make sure we have multifactor authentication so that even if a password is stolen the hacker can’t immediately gain access to the user’s account.
	Callego has basic encryption for web traffic and some of the databases. We don’t have standardized protection across all systems. We also have nothing in the way of data loss prevention controls. In the future we need to have standard controls across all places that data could be stored. We should also classify the types of data that we have so that we can make sure that we secure the highly sensitive data first and add data loss controls. 
Systematic Functionality Explanation
	The three types of controls that I suggest work well together because they form a layered security system. Role Based access controls along with having multifactor authentication helps to make sure only the correct people are accessing data. This is the first layer of security. Next, we have encryption setup. This way even if someone gets access to the data they can’t read it. Finally, the data loss prevention system we put in place monitors the data to make sure it isn’t being lost or misused. A hacker would have to work his way through all three layers of protection before gaining anything useful from the data.
Information Security Principle Explanation
	Role-based access directly supports the principles of least privilege. The employees only have access to what is necessary to complete their jobs. Role-based access also supports data minimization because limiting access also reduces the amount of information the employee can access. Encryption also supports the GDPR’s confidentiality and integrity principles. This would be an example of defense in depth since it is a second layer of protection against personal data being read. The SIEM with endpoint detection is also an example of defense in depth and layering. These controls add detection and preventive controls to the layers of security. Having these layers is important because you can’t guarantee that any single layer of security can prevent every breach.
Justification
	Role-based access along with multi-factor authentication can be implemented quickly and at fairly low cost. The total cost to implement these two is far less than a single data breach is likely to cost. I would implement this first. Encryption should also be implemented immediately, starting with the most sensitive data. Callego already uses cloud platforms and encryption capabilities may already be present on the platform making it easy to implement and cost effective. Implementing a full SIEM would probably take a lot longer and be more expensive. If everything we use is on a cloud-based system, then implementation would be much quicker. This would be the last control I would implement due to time and money but would still try to get it done before the new partnership begins. Role-based access along with multi-factor authentication and encryption are key and should all be able to be implemented easily even with the accelerated time frame.
Milestone Three
Callego Tabletop IVA Simulation
Introduction
	Intelligent Virtual Assistants (IVAS) are starting to be seen everywhere. When you go to a bank website or Amazon there is a Virtual Assistant that tries to help you. They are being set up all these places because it is cheaper than hiring people to do these roles and more efficient. There are a lot of good things and bad things when it comes to IVAS. With technology advancing so fast people don’t have time to find all the cybersecurity risks that are introduced with these new technologies. IVAS can interact with users, access internal systems, get information from databases and other things that help users. Due to this if an IVA is compromised it could cause serious issues.
	The tabletop exercise that we will be doing at Callego will focus on security risks associated with Intelligent Virtual Assistants. The exercise will simulate an incident in which an attacker gets an IVA to provide them with unauthorized access to sensitive information. The reason I wanted to do this exercise is because Callego is trying to create their own IVA that will help customers. We need to know what risks are associated with this new IVA that is being named SONYA and how we can make her as safe as possible.
	Doing a tabletop simulation will allow us to find critical flaws in system integration and in how the AI works with humans. This simulation may allow humans to identify if our IVA is giving false information. Another benefit is that we can decide when and how our IVA SONYA hands off customers to people. Another benefit is that we may be able to find weaknesses such as injections where hackers try to trick the AI into giving away confidential information.
Objectives
	The main objective of this exercise is to determine how effectively Callego staff can manage a cybersecurity incident that involves an IVA. We need to be able to identify suspicious behavior performed by the IVA. We need to make sure that the technical controls in place keep SONYA from accessing things she shouldn’t. We also need to make sure Callegos incident response and communication procedures work effectively in this type of situation. Finally, we need to try to identify weaknesses in policies, procedures, and technical controls. 
Team roles and responsibilities
	Since this is a simulation, I want to keep the number of roles to a minimum so that the exercise runs smoothly. There needs to be roles that deal with incident response and roles that deal with legal and business issues. With these three types of roles, we should be able to meet our objectives. 
	The first role will be the incident response lead He will be a participate and his responsibilities will include declaring and managing the incident as well as establishing response priorities. He will also make sure that each role is going to the expert in that subject. He will also communicate with all of them and will be in charge of escalating to senior management if he deems it necessary.
	The next role is going to be the technical lead. This is also a participant role. He will review security alerts and analyze what the IVA is doing. Other responsibilities include identifying attack vectors and recommending containment actions. After he has done all of those things he also needs to preserve technical evidence in case something needs to be brought before the law.
	The next role would be an IVA engineer. This is a participant role.  This person either worked on the IVA or at least understands how it works. He will be in charge of monitoring the IVAs behaviors and making sure it doesn’t access things it shouldn’t. He will also decide if or when the IVA should be disabled if there is an incident. Then he will work on restoring the IVA and fixing any issues with that IVA.
	After that we need a compliance representative. This person determines if any personal data has been exposed in the case of an incident. They also advise the team about when notifications need to be sent out to anyone. Other responsibilities of theirs are ensuring proper documentation is maintained and coordinating with legal personnel if needed.
	Finally, I think a joint role of communications and business lead is needed.  This is also a participant role.  All information ends up at this person so he can communicate to whoever is necessary, such as senior management or stakeholders. This person also determines the business impact of disabling the IVA and has a plan in place to minimize workflow issues if this happens. I will be the facilitator and run the participants through the simulation. Observers’ roles will be filled in by all other staff that are wanting to watch. 
Elements to be tested
	For this simulation the main security principle that I want to test is data leakage. Calligo’s IVA SONYA should only have enough access to things to perform necessary functions. We want to make sure that SONYA doesn’t share things she shouldn’t have access to.
	There are a lot of security policies and procedures required to make sure that SONYA and anyone working with her are doing what they are supposed to do. A policy that the participants must determine is when SONYA should be disabled. This is very important because it will have a major impact on Callego’s business. The procedure that should go with this is how they go about disabling her. Can they do it in steps or just disable certain parts of SONYA to have the smallest effect on the business? These are things that the participants, especially the ones assigned to these roles, need to determine. 
	Logging and monitoring IVA activities is the control measure that we will be testing during this simulation. The participants will have access to IVA interaction and authentication logs. They will also be able to see network activity and all security alerts. The participants will use this information to make sure that SONYA is displaying normal behaviors.
	The main countermeasure we will use is containment of the IVA SONYA. There are several different ways that we can try to contain SONYA. We could completely disable her but that could cost the company a lot of money since customers would not be able to access things they need. We could also choose to limit what she has access to for now or simply try to block suspicious activity. The participants need to decide how to proceed and each choice will have different consequences such as money or reputation loss for Callego.
Exercise Timeline
	This exercise starts at 9am. Every hour there will be new things introduced into the exercise with an hour-long break for lunch at noon. This will continue until we finish the entire exercise. If it seems like people are needing breaks we will allow for a 15 minute break as needed. The initial attack vector comes from a user who seems to be normal at first. After interacting with SONYA for a while this user starts to make requests that seem to be testing what things SONYA has access to. Then the requests become more malicious in intent as the user makes requests that are specifically designed to make SONYA ignore her restrictions and access data that she normally can’t.
	This attack tests the principles, procedures, and control measures that are in place.  By asking SONYA to access things, she doesn’t need to do her job, it is testing the security principle of least privilege.  When the participants get an incident report they will have to go through the procedure of disabling SONYA. This incident will also test the control measures by seeing how well the monitoring and logging system picks up on abnormal behavior. The participants will need to decide if behavior is normal and if not, what they should do about it?
	There are a lot of consequences that could happen based on the decisions that the participants make. If they disable SONYA right away the suspicious activity will stop but investigators will have limited information about the attack. The participants then would need to decide how to investigate with this limited amount of information. 
	The participants could choose instead to just monitor the attack. The consequence of this is that SONYA may end up revealing sensitive data. Then participants would need to decide if the risk is too great to continue monitoring and if they should shut SONYA down. 
	The first thing that I will inject into the scenario is that 5,000 customer records were revealed that contain names and contact information. I would ask does this change how you are responding to the situation? Who needs to be notified now that sensitive data has been revealed? Should SONYA now be disabled to protect other customers privacy?
	Now the attacker seems to be trying to shut down Callego’s systems. Now as the facilitator I would ask, do we handle this differently than when they were just trying to steal sensitive data? Do we need to inform leadership about this change? What is the best way to handle this new situation? Do we disable all access to SONYA?



Lessons Learned
	The technical controls that we use for monitoring activities will probably be able to see that there is unusual activity but may not be able to determine exactly what that activity is or what was accessed. We could address this issue by setting up specific monitoring systems for individual databases. We also need to make sure that least privilege is implemented and changed depending on what we determine SONYA actually needs access to. 
	Callego needs to have clear incident-escalation rules in place. This way they know who informs management of incidents. There also needs to be a person who communicates with customers. A person who handles the legal and compliance issues should also be looped in on any communication that reaches management level.
Training and Awareness
	The first thing that should be trained on is on how to recognize suspicious IVA behavior. Next, everyone should be trained on whom to report to. Training should also include how social engineering attacks can target IVAS. Technical people should then go on to learn how the logging and access control are set up for SONYA. They should learn about prompt manipulation and evidence preservation. All these training should be gone over every 6 months since the way hackers attack is always changing. 
Regulatory and Legal Compliance
	The exercise should determine if the participants can identify unusual IVA behavior. They should also know what constitutes a data-breach. They should know what must be documented and how to do so. They also need to know when legal personnel should become involved.
Documenting Risk
	 The participants should learn how to document technical issues in a way that management can understand. This means they should explain what happened and how, what systems were affected, and if personal information was exposed. Then they need to explain what they did to contain or fix the issue. Finally, they need to explain what risk remains.






